The {{nihongo|'''Okami Metro'''|岡見メトロ|Okami Metoro}} is a major [[rapid transit system]] in the [[Okami|Okami Metropolitan Area]] of [[Okaiken]], operated by [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]], and originally opened in 1935 as the Okami Municipal Subway.

Okami Metro forms an integral part of the extensive mass transit system of Greater Okami, operating 13 main subway lines and one automated guideway transit line. As of 2026, the network transports approximately 4.98 million passengers daily, functioning as the primary urban transport backbone for [[Okami]] and connecting it to surrounding municipalities through its connections with many private rail operators in the area.

Okami Metro is legally classified partially under the Tram Act and partially under the Railway Business Act, though it operates entirely as a heavy rail metro system. Major routes include the [[Miyoshi Line]], which serves as the primary north-south artery beneath Miyoshi Boulevard, and the [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]], which bisects the city east-to-west. The system is closely integrated with regional rail networks, including [[Okaiken Railways]] (OR) and private railways such as [[Okaden|Okami Electric Railway (Okaden)]], allowing for seamless through-service on several lines.

Following the privatization of the [[Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau]] on April 1, 2018, the system has undergone significant modernization, including the introduction of the Satori-AI integrated passenger management system and the rollout of new, modern rolling stock.

==History==
In 1905, the Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau began operating its municipal tram system. As the Imperial capital's industry expanded rapidly following the [[Sanese-Relastovan War]], the streetcar network grew to cover most of the city. However, by the 1920s, surface congestion necessitated a grade-separated solution.

Construction of the first subway line began in 1930. The first section of what is now the [[Miyoshi Line]] opened in June 1935, operating between [[Okami Chūō Station|Okami Chūō]] and [[Hozumi-Oiwake Station|Hozumi Oiwake]]. It was the first publicly operated subway in the Mitoyagi Empire. Construction to connect the Miyoshi Line to the densely inhabited island of [[Tamashima]] was halted during the [[Great Adonian War]] but resumed aggressively in the 1960s to support the city's post-war economic miracle.

By 1970, a grid of six lines had been established. In the 1990s and 2000s, newer lines utilizing [[linear motor]] technology were introduced to serve the denser, older districts of the city where deep-bore tunneling was required.

On April 1, 2018, the system was privatized as Okami Metro Co., Ltd., ending over 80 years of direct municipal management.

==Lines==
Currently, there are 13 subway lines and one [[automated guideway transit]] (AGT) line known as the "New Tram." The system spans {{convert|168.4|km|mi|sp=us}} of track and serves 148 stations.

A key feature of the Okami Metro is its extensive through-service agreements. The [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]], for example, connects directly with the [[Okaiken Railways]] commuter lines, allowing passengers to travel from the western suburbs to the eastern industrial zones without changing trains. Other lines that offer through connection include the Nanboku Line with the [[Akitetsu|Akitetsu Railway]] and the Okaiken Railways, and the [[Tokuyama Line (Okami Metro)|Tokuyama Line]] with the Okaiken Railways and the [[Nankai Main Line|Nankai Line]].

